  "query_preview": "..A large area of low pressure remains centered \nacross the Pacific Northwest. This trough will gradually descend\nalong the West Coast through the middle of the week, before\nlifting towards the weekend. This will keep Arizona under a\nsouthwesterly flow aloft, allowing sufficient moisture to \ncontinue pumping into the state. Plus, this pattern will keep \neffective dynamics in place for a severe thunderstorm threat. \nAlthough we are not expecting widespread activity for today \n(Tuesday), hi-res guidance suggests greatest coverage of \nconvection to be across the Mogollon Rim and northward. There \ncould be a delayed start to activity due to a few showers/storms \nthat are occurring during the overnight hours tonight. Most \nactivity is across northern Gila County and the White Mountains \nat the moment, but this could easily work over the environment and\nlead way to debris clouds across the area after sunrise. Areas \nthat see cloud cover burn off and warm up, will likely be where \nstorms/showers fire up first. \n\nThis type of synoptic pattern leads way to greater chances for\novernight convection. Hi-res guidance has been consistent with a\nlarge complex of showers/storms pushing up into our CWA Wednesday\nmorning, and remaining fairly active throughout the \nafternoon/early evening. For what it's worth, guidance over the \nlast few days hasn't been the most consistent, so we'll see what \nupcoming runs say as well. Either way, we'll remain under an \nactive pattern through the end of the week. \n\nWe could see a brief break in activity over the weekend due to the\npreviously mentioned trough lifting and allowing a ridge to build\nover the region. There will probably be a few isolated\nshowers/storms remaining across the White Mountains, but overall\nshould be drier this weekend than what we've seen this week. \n\nAnother trough looks to impact the area early next week, so more\nunsettled weather may return. Stay tuned.",
